<p>In the conventional FASTQ layout, each record has a header beginning with <code>@</code>, a sequence, a <code>+</code> separator, and a quality string of matching length. A nonzero line-count remainder is a warning, not a complete parser-level validation. This quick check assumes unwrapped four-line records; malformed wrapping can invalidate that assumption. <code>pipefail</code> makes a decompression failure visible through the pipeline status in common shells such as Bash.</p>
<p>For paired-end data, compare record counts and read-name identifiers in R1 and R2. Equal counts alone do not show that mates are in the same order or correctly paired. The R-first check below reads both gzip-compressed files in lockstep, verifies four-line records and equal sequence/quality lengths, and compares the first header token after removing a terminal <code>/1</code> or <code>/2</code>. It assumes the remaining token is identical between mates; if your provider uses a different naming convention (for example, mate labels in a second header field), inspect its documentation and adapt the normalization rather than accepting a mismatch blindly.</p>
<h3 id="fastq-mates-r-route">R: compare paired read identifiers</h3>
<p>Save this base R script as <code>check_mates.R</code> and run <code>Rscript --vanilla check_mates.R reads_R1.fastq.gz reads_R2.fastq.gz</code>. It streams one four-line record at a time rather than loading both files into memory.</p>
<pre id="fastq-mates-r"><code class="language-r">args &lt;- commandArgs(trailingOnly = TRUE)
if (length(args) != 2L) stop(&quot;Usage: Rscript check_mates.R R1.fastq.gz R2.fastq.gz&quot;, call. = FALSE)
check_mates &lt;- function(paths) {
  handles &lt;- lapply(paths, gzfile, open = &quot;rt&quot;)
  on.exit(lapply(handles, close), add = TRUE)
  next_id &lt;- function(handle, path) {
    block &lt;- readLines(handle, n = 4L, warn = FALSE)
    if (!length(block)) return(NULL)
    if (length(block) != 4L) stop(path, &quot;: incomplete four-line record&quot;, call. = FALSE)
    if (!startsWith(block[[1]], &quot;@&quot;) || !startsWith(block[[3]], &quot;+&quot;) ||
        nchar(block[[2]], type = &quot;bytes&quot;) != nchar(block[[4]], type = &quot;bytes&quot;)) {
      stop(path, &quot;: malformed record near &quot;, block[[1]], call. = FALSE)
    }
    token &lt;- sub(&quot;[[:space:]].*$&quot;, &quot;&quot;, substring(block[[1]], 2L))
    if (!nzchar(token)) stop(path, &quot;: missing read identifier&quot;, call. = FALSE)
    sub(&quot;/[12]$&quot;, &quot;&quot;, token)
  }
  n &lt;- 0L
  repeat {
    a &lt;- next_id(handles[[1]], paths[[1]])
    b &lt;- next_id(handles[[2]], paths[[2]])
    if (is.null(a) &amp;&amp; is.null(b)) break
    n &lt;- n + 1L
    if (is.null(a) || is.null(b)) stop(&quot;FAIL: different record counts at record &quot;, n, call. = FALSE)
    if (!identical(a, b)) stop(&quot;FAIL: mate identifiers/order differ at record &quot;, n, call. = FALSE)
  }
  if (n) cat(sprintf(&quot;PASS: %d paired records; normalized identifiers match in order\n&quot;, n))
  else cat(&quot;PASS: both files are empty\n&quot;)
}
check_mates(args)</code></pre>
